  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{(x+4)({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-5x)}{{x}^{2}+4x}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-5x)}{{x}^{2}+4x} & =\\frac{x(x+4)({x}^{2}+3x-5)}{x(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x(x+4)}({x}^{2}+3x-5)}{\\cancel{x(x+4)}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/a10cef51ad5955f6065fbf4deaccbab1cda1f576db96310236ea976bb8d8eb8b.png)",
